WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 10.11 · Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk
Risk registers, ownership, mitigation and escalation

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Start with ten to fifteen risks, each with one owner, a response and an escalation route.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.281
A risk register keeps your risks, scores, owners, actions and review dates in one place. A new initiative might start with ten to fifteen risks and add more as it grows.

4
00:00:26.681 --> 00:00:27.281
Four ways to respond

5
00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:37.870
Avoid: Stop or change the activity, so the risk no longer exists. For example: The bakery drops night baking for trainees and keeps all training to daytime.

6
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:46.978
Reduce: Add controls that lower the likelihood or the impact. For example: A safety checklist completed before every shift in the shared kitchen.

7
00:00:46.978 --> 00:00:57.567
Transfer: Pass some of the impact to others through insurance or contracts. For example: Public liability insurance covers claims if a customer is hurt at the stall.

8
00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:08.244
Accept: Live with the risk, if it is within your risk appetite. For example: The bakery accepts that a new recipe may not sell.

9
00:01:08.644 --> 00:01:09.244
Why owners and limits matter

10
00:01:09.244 --> 00:01:14.548
1. One owner per risk means someone actually acts on it.

11
00:01:14.548 --> 00:01:20.963
2. Risk appetite shows where you can try new ideas and where you cannot.

12
00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:27.267
3. Clear escalation means serious risks reach the right people fast.

13
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:28.267
What you will set

14
00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:33.941
1. An owner for each risk, shared out across the founding team.

15
00:01:33.941 --> 00:01:39.615
2. Your risk appetite: very low for safeguarding, legal and financial integrity.

16
00:01:39.615 --> 00:01:47.400
3. Escalation rules: which scores go to both founders, an adviser and later a board.

17
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:48.400
What to avoid

18
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.963
A register so long that nobody uses it.

19
00:01:52.963 --> 00:01:56.415
One founder owning every risk.

20
00:01:56.415 --> 00:02:01.607
Actions with no owner or due date.

21
00:02:02.207 --> 00:02:04.007
You build your first risk register with owners, responses and escalation.

22
00:02:04.007 --> 00:02:08.007
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:12.607
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
